  "id": "Compound::DB00945 - Gene::1557",
  "verbalization": "Acetylsalicylic Acid (ASA) is shown to bind to Cyp2c9, a cytochrome P450 enzyme involved in the metabolism of various substrates, including limonene. This binding is supported by multiple paths, all of which indicate a direct interaction between ASA and Cyp2c9, which in turn participates in the activity of limonene monooxygenase. The shared involvement of Cyp2c9 in the metabolism of limonene, albeit through different isoforms (6- and 7-monooxygenase), suggests a common functional role in the oxidative activation of limonene. The consistent scores across the three paths (final_score=0.7675) indicate a strong, reliable relationship between ASA and Cyp2c19, as the enzyme is directly implicated in the activity of limonene monooxygenase. The mechanism of action is thus likely mediated through the inhibition of Cyp2c9, which would affect the metabolism of limonene and other substrates. The evidence supports a direct, mechanistic link between ASA and Cyp2c19, with the enzyme playing a central role in the metabolic pathway of limonene.",
